  20CA'   00                    	DB	0
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-101


                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  20CB'                         EXADDR:
  20CB'   AF                    	XRA	A
  20CC'   32 273A'              	STA	SRNGF
  20CF'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-102


  20D0'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  20D2'   CA 20FB'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  20D5'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
  20D6'   CD 2110'              	CALL	EXNUM
  20D9'   DA 20F9'              	JC	EXA_NO
  20DC'   22 273B'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  20DF'   22 273D'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  20E2'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  20E3'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  20E5'   C2 20F2'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  20E8'   13                    	INX	D
  20E9'   CD 2110'              	CALL	EXNUM
  20EC'   DA 20F9'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
  20EF'   22 273D'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  20F2'                         EXA_YS:
  20F2'   EB                    	XCHG
  20F3'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  20F5'   32 273A'              	STA	SRNGF
  20F8'   C9                    	RET
  20F9'                         EXA_NO:
  20F9'   EB                    	XCHG
  20FA'   C9                    	RET
  20FB'                         EXA_AL:
  20FB'   23                    	INX	H
  20FC'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  20FD'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2100'   22 273B'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  2103'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  2106'   22 273D'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  2109'   E1                    	POP	H
  210A'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  210C'   32 273A'              	STA	SRNGF
  210F'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
                                ;digit there at all.
  2110'                         EXNUM:
  2110'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  2111'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  2113'   D8                    	RC
  2114'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  2116'   3F                    	CMC
  2117'   D8                    	RC
  2118'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  211B'                         EXN_LP:
  211B'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  211C'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  211E'   DA 2138'              	JC	EXN_DN
  2121'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  2123'   D2 2138'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  2126'   13                    	INX	D
  2127'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2128'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  2129'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  212B'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  212C'   54                    	MOV	D,H
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-103


  212D'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  212E'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  212F'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
  2130'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  2131'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  2132'   D1                    	POP	D
  2133'   09                    	DAD	B
  2134'   C1                    	POP	B
  2135'   C3 211B'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  2138'                         EXN_DN:
  2138'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  2139'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  213A'                         EX_S:
  213A'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  213B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  213C'   CA 217E'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  213F'   32 273F'              	STA	SDELIM
  2142'   23                    	INX	H
  2143'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2146'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  2149'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  214A'   CD 2184'              	CALL	SCOPY
  214D'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  214E'   B7                    	ORA	A
  214F'   CA 217E'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  2152'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  2155'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2157'   32 270B'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  215A'   11 274C'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  215D'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  215F'   CD 2184'              	CALL	SCOPY
  2162'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2163'   32 274B'              	STA	SREPLN
  2166'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2167'   32 2740'              	STA	SGFLAG
  216A'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  216B'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
  216D'   C2 2176'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  2170'   23                    	INX	H
  2171'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2173'   32 2740'              	STA	SGFLAG
  2176'                         EX_SG:
  2176'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2177'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2178'   C2 1F4D'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
  217B'   C3 21A4'              	JMP	SUBGO
  217E'                         SUBNOP:
  217E'   21 1FF9'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  2181'   C3 1F5B'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-104


  2184'                         SCOPY:
  2184'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  2186'                         SCP_LP:
  2186'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2187'   B9                    	CMP	C
  2188'   D2 21A1'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  218B'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  218C'   B7                    	ORA	A
  218D'   CA 21A1'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  2190'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2191'   21 273F'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
  2194'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2195'   E1                    	POP	H
  2196'   CA 21A0'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  2199'   12                    	STAX	D
  219A'   13                    	INX	D
  219B'   23                    	INX	H
  219C'   04                    	INR	B
  219D'   C3 2186'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  21A0'                         SCP_SK:
  21A0'   23                    	INX	H
  21A1'                         SCP_DN:
  21A1'   AF                    	XRA	A
  21A2'   12                    	STAX	D
  21A3'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  21A4'                         SUBGO:
  21A4'   CD 0EBF'              	CALL	GZONE
  21A7'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  21AA'   22 2749'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  21AD'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
  21B0'   22 2747'              	SHLD	SOFFS		;   search's SN_BAD does
  21B3'   AF                    	XRA	A
  21B4'   32 2741'              	STA	SANY
  21B7'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  21BA'   22 2743'              	SHLD	SBACK
  21BD'   3A 273A'              	LDA	SRNGF
  21C0'   B7                    	ORA	A
  21C1'   CA 21EA'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  21C4'   2A 273B'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  21C7'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  21C8'   B5                    	ORA	L
  21C9'   CA 2266'              	JZ	SUBBAD		; ':0s' -- there is no line 0
  21CC'   2A 273D'              	LHLD	SRNG2
  21CF'   EB                    	XCHG
  21D0'   2A 273B'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  21D3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; CY iff SRNG2 < SRNG1
  21D6'   DA 2266'              	JC	SUBBAD		; backwards: vim offers to swap, this refuses
  21D9'   EB                    	XCHG			; HL = the last line, DE = the first
  21DA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD
  21DD'   23                    	INX	H		; HL = the lines in the range
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-105


  21DE'   22 2745'              	SHLD	SLINES
  21E1'   2A 273B'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  21E4'   CD 0E40'              	CALL	GOLN
  21E7'   C3 21F0'              	JMP	SUBLP
  21EA'                         SUB_1L:
  21EA'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  21ED'   22 2745'              	SHLD	SLINES
  21F0'                         SUBLP:
  21F0'   CD 226C'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  21F3'   B7                    	ORA	A
  21F4'   CA 2202'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  21F7'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  21F9'   32 2741'              	STA	SANY
  21FC'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  21FF'   22 2743'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  2202'                         SUB_NX:
  2202'   2A 2745'              	LHLD	SLINES
  2205'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2206'   22 2745'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2209'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  220A'   B5                    	ORA	L
  220B'   CA 2229'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  220E'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2211'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2212'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  2215'   3A 2741'              	LDA	SANY
  2218'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2219'   CA 2223'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
  221C'   2A 2743'              	LHLD	SBACK
  221F'   23                    	INX	H
  2220'   22 2743'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  2223'                         SUB_AE:
  2223'   CD 0985'              	CALL	ATEOF
  2226'   D2 21F0'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  2229'                         SUBDON:
  2229'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  222C'   3A 2741'              	LDA	SANY
  222F'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2230'   CA 224E'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  2233'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2235'   32 268C'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
  2238'   2A 2743'              	LHLD	SBACK
  223B'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  223C'   B5                    	ORA	L
  223D'   CA 2248'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  2240'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  2242'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  2245'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  2248'                         SUB_PL:
  2248'   CD 0ED7'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  224B'   C3 06FC'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-106


                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  224E'                         SUBNFD:
  224E'   2A 2747'              	LHLD	SOFFS
  2251'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
  2254'   2A 2749'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  2257'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
  225A'   21 1FE5'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  225D'   CD 1F5B'              	CALL	EXERR
  2260'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2263'   C3 1F56'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  2266'                         SUBBAD:
  2266'   21 1FD3'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  2269'   C3 1F50'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
  226C'                         SUBLIN:
  226C'   AF                    	XRA	A
  226D'   32 2742'              	STA	SLCHG
  2270'   CD 22EB'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  2273'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  2276'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  2276'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  2279'   DA 2281'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  227C'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  227E'   C2 2276'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  2281'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  2281'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2284'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2285'   CD 13D3'              	CALL	CEND
  2288'   D1                    	POP	D
  2289'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
  228C'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  228D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  2290'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  2291'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  2292'   FA 22E4'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
  2295'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  2296'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  2297'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-107


  229A'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  229D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
  22A0'   DA 22E4'              	JC	SUBL_D
  22A3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  22A6'   EB                    	XCHG
  22A7'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  22AA'   EB                    	XCHG
  22AB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  22AE'   EB                    	XCHG
  22AF'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
  22B0'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  22B3'   D1                    	POP	D
  22B4'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  22B5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  22B8'   3A 2741'              	LDA	SANY
  22BB'   B7                    	ORA	A
  22BC'   C2 22C9'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  22BF'   3A 2742'              	LDA	SLCHG
  22C2'   B7                    	ORA	A
  22C3'   C2 22C9'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  22C6'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  22C9'                         SUBL_C:
  22C9'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
  22CC'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  22CD'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  22CF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  22D2'   11 274B'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
  22D5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  22D8'                         SUBL_I:
  22D8'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  22DA'   32 2742'              	STA	SLCHG
  22DD'   3A 2740'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  22E0'   B7                    	ORA	A
  22E1'   C2 2281'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
  22E4'                         SUBL_D:
  22E4'   CD 22EB'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  22E7'   3A 2742'              	LDA	SLCHG
  22EA'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
  22EB'                         SUBBOL:
  22EB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  22EE'   CD 05C7'              	CALL	BOL
  22F1'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
  22F4'                         VC_SRCH:
  22F4'   CD 06DB'              	CALL	SETMOV
  22F7'   C5                    	PUSH	B
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-108


  22F8'   CD 066E'              	CALL	GETCNT
  22FB'   22 2733'              	SHLD	SCNT
  22FE'   C1                    	POP	B
  22FF'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2300'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  2302'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  2304'   CA 2309'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  2307'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  2309'                         VS_DIR:
  2309'   32 2709'              	STA	SDIR
  230C'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  230F'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  2312'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2313'   CD 1AAB'              	CALL	RDLINE
  2316'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2317'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  2318'   3A 26E1'              	LDA	RLLEN
  231B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  231C'   CA 2331'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  231F'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  2320'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  2322'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2325'   19                    	DAD	D
  2326'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  2328'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  2329'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  232C'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  232E'   32 270B'              	STA	SHAVE
  2331'                         VS_OLD:
  2331'   3A 270B'              	LDA	SHAVE
  2334'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2335'   CA 2341'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  2338'   3A 2709'              	LDA	SDIR
  233B'   32 270A'              	STA	SDIRE
  233E'   C3 236A'              	JMP	SRCHN
  2341'                         VS_NOP:
  2341'   21 1FF9'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  2344'   C3 1F5B'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  2347'                         VC_N:
  2347'   CD 06DB'              	CALL	SETMOV
  234A'   3A 2709'              	LDA	SDIR
  234D'   C3 2357'              	JMP	VN_GO
  2350'                         VCB_N:
  2350'   CD 06DB'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2353'   3A 2709'              	LDA	SDIR
  2356'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  2357'                         VN_GO:
  2357'   32 270A'              	STA	SDIRE
  235A'   CD 066E'              	CALL	GETCNT
  235D'   22 2733'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2360'   3A 270B'              	LDA	SHAVE
  2363'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2364'   CA 2341'              	JZ	VS_NOP
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-109


  2367'   C3 236A'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  236A'                         SRCHN:
  236A'   CD 0EBF'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  236D'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  2370'   22 2737'              	SHLD	SLDLT		; the sweep moves the cursor with PUTCUR, which
                                				;   does not maintain LNDLT (GOTO does)
  2373'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2376'   22 2735'              	SHLD	SOFF
  2379'                         SN_LP:
  2379'   CD 23B1'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  237C'   DA 2396'              	JC	SN_BAD
  237F'   2A 2733'              	LHLD	SCNT
  2382'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2383'   22 2733'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2386'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2387'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2388'   C2 2379'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  238B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  238E'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2390'   32 2716'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
  2393'   C3 0ED7'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  2396'                         SN_BAD:
  2396'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  2399'   2A 2735'              	LHLD	SOFF
  239C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
  239F'   2A 2737'              	LHLD	SLDLT
  23A2'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  23A5'   21 1FE5'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  23A8'   CD 1F5B'              	CALL	EXERR
  23AB'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  23AE'   C3 1F56'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  23B1'                         SRCHGO:
  23B1'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  23B3'   32 2739'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  23B6'   CD 23C8'              	CALL	SRCHW
  23B9'   D0                    	RNC
  23BA'   3A 270A'              	LDA	SDIRE
  23BD'   2F                    	CMA
  23BE'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  23C1'   AF                    	XRA	A
  23C2'   32 2739'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  23C5'   C3 23C8'              	JMP	SRCHW
                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-110


                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  23C8'                         SRCHW:
  23C8'   3A 270A'              	LDA	SDIRE
  23CB'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  23CE'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  23D1'   22 2689'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
  23D4'   AF                    	XRA	A
  23D5'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  23D8'                         SW_LP:
  23D8'   CD 23E4'              	CALL	SRCH1
  23DB'   D0                    	RNC
  23DC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  23DF'   D2 23D8'              	JNC	SW_LP
  23E2'   37                    	STC
  23E3'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  23E4'                         SRCH1:
  23E4'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  23E7'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
  23EA'   B7                    	ORA	A
  23EB'   FA 240F'              	JM	SR1_BK
  23EE'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  23F1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  23F4'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  23F7'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  23F8'   3A 2739'              	LDA	SSKIP
  23FB'   B7                    	ORA	A
  23FC'   CA 2405'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  23FF'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2400'   B1                    	ORA	C
  2401'   37                    	STC
  2402'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  2403'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  2404'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
  2405'                         SR1_FW:
  2405'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  2408'   D8                    	RC
  2409'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  240C'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  240F'                         SR1_BK:
  240F'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
  2412'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2415'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  2418'   D8                    	RC
  2419'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-111


  241C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
  241F'   D8                    	RC
  2420'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  2423'                         MSGNAM:
  2423'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2424'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2426'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  2429'   E1                    	POP	H
  242A'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
  242B'   23                    	INX	H
  242C'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  242D'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  242F'   C2 243C'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  2432'   21 2067'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  2435'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2438'   E1                    	POP	H
  2439'   C3 2486'              	JMP	MN_END
  243C'                         MN_HAS:
  243C'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  243E'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  2441'   E1                    	POP	H
  2442'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2443'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
  2444'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2445'   CA 2453'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  2448'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2449'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  244B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  244E'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  2450'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2453'                         MN_NAM:
  2453'   E1                    	POP	H
  2454'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2455'   23                    	INX	H
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-112


  2456'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
  2458'                         MN_NM:
  2458'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2459'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  245B'   CA 2466'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  245E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2461'   23                    	INX	H
  2462'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2463'   C2 2458'              	JNZ	MN_NM
  2466'                         MN_EXT:
  2466'   E1                    	POP	H
  2467'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  246A'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
  246B'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  246C'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  246E'   CA 2486'              	JZ	MN_END
  2471'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  2473'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2476'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  2478'                         MN_XL:
  2478'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2479'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  247B'   CA 2486'              	JZ	MN_END
  247E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2481'   23                    	INX	H
  2482'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2483'   C2 2478'              	JNZ	MN_XL
  2486'                         MN_END:
  2486'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2488'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  248B'                         MSGFLG:
  248B'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  248D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  248E'   CA 249E'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2491'   3A 268C'              	LDA	MODF
  2494'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2495'   CA 249E'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2498'   21 2071'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  249B'   CD 24B3'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  249E'                         MF_RO:
  249E'   3A 26F7'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  24A1'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24A2'   CA 24AB'              	JZ	MF_NEW
  24A5'   21 207C'              	LXI	H,SROM
  24A8'   CD 24B3'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  24AB'                         MF_NEW:
  24AB'   3A 26F5'              	LDA	MSGNEW
  24AE'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24AF'   C8                    	RZ
  24B0'   21 2087'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  24B3'                         MF_PUT:
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-113


  24B3'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  24B4'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24B5'   C2 24C1'              	JNZ	MF_STR
  24B8'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  24BA'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  24BB'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  24BD'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  24C0'   E1                    	POP	H
  24C1'                         MF_STR:
  24C1'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  24C4'                         EMITW:
  24C4'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  24C5'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  24C6'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  24C8'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  24CB'   CD 24E9'              	CALL	EWDIG
  24CE'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  24D1'   CD 24E9'              	CALL	EWDIG
  24D4'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
  24D7'   CD 24E9'              	CALL	EWDIG
  24DA'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  24DD'   CD 24E9'              	CALL	EWDIG
  24E0'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
  24E1'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  24E3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  24E6'   D1                    	POP	D
  24E7'   C1                    	POP	B
  24E8'   C9                    	RET
  24E9'                         EWDIG:
  24E9'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  24EB'                         EW_LP:
  24EB'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  24EC'   93                    	SUB	E
  24ED'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  24EE'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  24EF'   9A                    	SBB	D
  24F0'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  24F1'   DA 24F8'              	JC	EW_DN
  24F4'   0C                    	INR	C
  24F5'   C3 24EB'              	JMP	EW_LP
  24F8'                         EW_DN:
  24F8'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  24F9'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  24FA'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24FB'   C2 2501'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  24FE'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  24FF'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2500'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
  2501'                         EW_PR:
  2501'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2502'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2504'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-114


  2507'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2509'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  250A'                         ISEMPT:
  250A'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  250D'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  250E'   B5                    	ORA	L
  250F'   C0                    	RNZ
  2510'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  2513'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2514'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2515'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  2516'                         MSGCTG:
  2516'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2519'   CD 2423'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  251C'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
  251E'   CD 248B'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  2521'   CD 250A'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  2524'   C2 252D'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  2527'   21 209A'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
  252A'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  252D'                         MG_POS:
  252D'   21 208D'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  2530'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2533'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  2536'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  2537'   CD 24C4'              	CALL	EMITW
  253A'   21 2094'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  253D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2540'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  2543'   23                    	INX	H
  2544'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2545'   CD 24C4'              	CALL	EMITW
  2548'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  254B'   23                    	INX	H
  254C'   D1                    	POP	D
  254D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  2550'   C8                    	RZ
  2551'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2552'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  2554'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2557'   E1                    	POP	H
  2558'   C3 24C4'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  255B'                         FILMSG:
  255B'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-115


  255E'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2560'   C8                    	RZ
  2561'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2564'   CD 2423'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2567'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  2568'   C3 248B'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  256B'                         WRMSG:
  256B'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  256E'   CD 2423'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2571'   21 20AC'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  2574'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
  2577'                         MSGPST:
  2577'   3A 26F4'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  257A'   B7                    	ORA	A
  257B'   C8                    	RZ
  257C'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  257D'   AF                    	XRA	A
  257E'   32 26F4'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2581'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2582'   FE 01                 	CPI	MW_FIL
  2584'   CA 25A0'              	JZ	MP_FIL
  2587'   FE 02                 	CPI	MW_WRT
  2589'   CA 25A6'              	JZ	MP_WRT
  258C'   FE 04                 	CPI	MW_BIG
  258E'   CA 2597'              	JZ	MP_BIG
  2591'   CD 2516'              	CALL	MSGCTG
  2594'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2597'                         MP_BIG:
  2597'   21 2048'              	LXI	H,SQBIG
  259A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXMSG
  259D'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  25A0'                         MP_FIL:
  25A0'   CD 255B'              	CALL	FILMSG
  25A3'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  25A6'                         MP_WRT:
  25A6'   CD 256B'              	CALL	WRMSG
  25A9'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
                                
                                ;VC_CTLG -- '^G'.  It changes nothing and moves nothing, so the frame is a
                                ; still one and the message goes on after it.
  25AC'                         VC_CTLG:
  25AC'   3E 03                 	MVI	A,MW_CTG
  25AE'   32 26F4'              	STA	MSGWHT
  25B1'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,R_MOVE
  25B3'   32 268D'              	STA	RINTENT
  25B6'   C9                    	RET
                                
  25B7'                         CMDTAB:
  25B7'   07                    	DB	07H			; ^G -- where am I?
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-116


  25B8'   25AC'                 	DW	VC_CTLG
  25BA'   68                    	DB	'h'
  25BB'   0734'                 	DW	VC_H
  25BD'   6C                    	DB	'l'
  25BE'   075E'                 	DW	VC_L
  25C0'   6A                    	DB	'j'
  25C1'   0823'                 	DW	VC_J
  25C3'   6B                    	DB	'k'
  25C4'   0832'                 	DW	VC_K
  25C6'   30                    	DB	'0'
  25C7'   0785'                 	DW	VC_0
  25C9'   5E                    	DB	'^'
  25CA'   07A3'                 	DW	VC_CRT
  25CC'   24                    	DB	'$'
  25CD'   07E1'                 	DW	VC_EOL
  25CF'   0D                    	DB	CR			; <CR> -- down to the first non-blank
  25D0'   0805'                 	DW	VC_CR
  25D2'   77                    	DB	'w'
  25D3'   0A07'                 	DW	VC_W
  25D5'   62                    	DB	'b'
  25D6'   0A58'                 	DW	VC_B
  25D8'   65                    	DB	'e'
  25D9'   0BBD'                 	DW	VC_E
  25DB'   57                    	DB	'W'
  25DC'   0A0E'                 	DW	VCBGW
  25DE'   42                    	DB	'B'
  25DF'   0A5F'                 	DW	VCBGB
  25E1'   45                    	DB	'E'
  25E2'   0BC4'                 	DW	VCBGE
  25E4'   47                    	DB	'G'
  25E5'   0E1F'                 	DW	VC_G
  25E7'   67                    	DB	'g'
  25E8'   0E53'                 	DW	VC_LG
  25EA'   78                    	DB	'x'
  25EB'   139C'                 	DW	VC_X
  25ED'   5A                    	DB	'Z'
  25EE'   1B2B'                 	DW	VC_BZ
  25F0'   66                    	DB	'f'
  25F1'   0237'                 	DW	VC_FF
  25F3'   46                    	DB	'F'
  25F4'   023C'                 	DW	VC_FB
  25F6'   74                    	DB	't'
  25F7'   0241'                 	DW	VC_TF
  25F9'   54                    	DB	'T'
  25FA'   0246'                 	DW	VC_TB
  25FC'   3B                    	DB	';'
  25FD'   0262'                 	DW	VC_SEMI
